{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"language":"python","display_name":"Python 3","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3.6.6","mimetype":"text/x-python","codemirror_mode":{"name":"ipython","version":3},"pygments_lexer":"ipython3","nbconvert_exporter":"python","file_extension":".py"},"kaggle":{"accelerator":"none","dataSources":[],"isInternetEnabled":true,"language":"python","sourceType":"notebook","isGpuEnabled":false}},"nbformat_minor":4,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"source":"<a href=\"https://www.kaggle.com/code/rizviabiha/rag-2?scriptVersionId=366855822\" target=\"_blank\"><img align=\"left\" alt=\"Kaggle\" title=\"Open in Kaggle\" src=\"https://kaggle.com/static/images/open-in-kaggle.svg\"></a>","metadata":{},"cell_type":"markdown"},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Large Language Models (LLMs), Fine-Tuning, In-Context Learning and RAG\n\n## 1. How Do LLMs Store Knowledge?\n\nLarge Language Models (LLMs) are neural networks primarily based on the **Transformer architecture**. They contain millions or billions of parameters (weights and biases).\n\nDuring pre-training, an LLM is trained on a massive amount of text data. It learns language patterns, relationships between words, facts, concepts, and other statistical patterns.\n\n### Where is this knowledge stored?\n\nThe knowledge learned during pre-training is encoded in the model's parameters (weights and biases).\n\nThis is known as **Parametric Knowledge**.\n\n- Parametric knowledge is the information and patterns learned by a model during training.\n- It is stored implicitly in the model's parameters.\n- It is not stored as a conventional database where we can directly search for a particular fact.\n- The model uses its learned parameters to generate responses when given a prompt.\n\n### Can we access parametric knowledge?\n\nYes. We can interact with the knowledge encoded in an LLM by providing prompts.\n\n**Basic workflow:**\n\n1. We provide a prompt or question to an LLM.\n2. The model processes the prompt using its learned parameters.\n3. It generates a response based on the patterns and knowledge learned during training.\n\nHowever, relying only on parametric knowledge has some limitations.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Limitations of LLMs\n\nThere are several situations in which prompting an LLM using only its existing knowledge may not produce satisfactory results.\n\n### Condition 1: Private or Domain-Specific Data\n\nSuppose I am watching a lecture on YouTube and suddenly have a doubt about a particular point.\n\nI ask the LLM:\n\n\"Explain the concept discussed at 41:50 in this lecture.\"\n\nThe LLM might not be able to answer because:\n\n- It may not have access to the video.\n- The video might not have been included in its training data.\n- It does not automatically know the contents of my private documents, lectures, or datasets.\n\nAn LLM cannot answer questions about information it cannot access.\n\n### Condition 2: Recent or Frequently Changing Information\n\nLLMs are trained on data available up to a particular point in time.\n\nThis is commonly referred to as the **Knowledge Cutoff Date**.\n\nFor example:\n\nSuppose an LLM was trained on information available until January 2025. If we ask it about an event that happened in October 2026, it might not know about that event.\n\nSome LLM applications can access external tools such as web search or APIs to obtain updated information. However, this capability depends on the application and its available tools, not simply on the model itself.\n\n### Condition 3: Hallucination\n\nHallucination occurs when an LLM generates information that sounds convincing but is incorrect, unsupported, or fabricated.\n\nFor example:\n\n- Providing a fake research paper or reference.\n- Generating incorrect technical explanations.\n- Giving outdated or inaccurate information confidently.\n\n**Why does hallucination happen?**\n\nLLMs generate responses by predicting the next token based on learned patterns and the available context. They do not automatically verify every generated statement against a reliable source.\n\nTherefore, an LLM can generate plausible-sounding but incorrect answers.\n\n### Can we overcome these limitations?\n\nTwo important techniques are:\n\n1. Fine-Tuning\n2. Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)\n\nBefore understanding RAG, let's understand fine-tuning and in-context learning.\n\n---\n\n# 3. Fine-Tuning\n\nFine-tuning is the process of taking a pre-trained model and training it further on a smaller, carefully selected dataset to improve its performance on a particular task or domain.\n\nInstead of training a model from scratch, we start with an already trained model and adapt it to our requirements.\n\n### Example\n\nSuppose we have a pre-trained LLM that understands general language but does not perform particularly well in the medical domain.\n\nWe can fine-tune it using a high-quality medical dataset containing medical questions, answers, terminology, and domain-specific examples.\n\nAfter fine-tuning, the model may become better at answering medical questions and following medical-domain instructions.\n\n**Important:** Fine-tuning teaches the model patterns, behaviour, and task-specific knowledge. It does not guarantee that the model will remember every training example or reliably reproduce every fact.\n\n## 3.1 Types and Techniques of Fine-Tuning\n\n### A. Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT)\n\nSupervised Fine-Tuning is a technique in which a pre-trained model is further trained using labelled examples consisting of inputs and their corresponding desired outputs.\n\nThe model learns to generate responses similar to the provided target answers.\n\n**Example:**\n\nTraining dataset:\n\n| Input (Question) | Expected Output |\n|---|---|\n| What is Python? | Python is a high-level programming language. |\n| What is SQL? | SQL is a language used to manage relational databases. |\n\nThe model is trained on these input-output pairs so that it learns to produce appropriate responses.\n\n**Use cases:**\n- Instruction following.\n- Chatbots.\n- Question-answering systems.\n- Domain-specific assistants.\n\n### B. Continued Pre-Training\n\nContinued pre-training involves taking an already pre-trained language model and training it further on additional large-scale, usually domain-specific, unlabelled text data.\n\nUnlike SFT, it does not necessarily require question-answer pairs.\n\nThe objective generally remains similar to the original language-model pre-training objective, such as predicting the next token.\n\n**Example:**\n\nSuppose we have a general-purpose LLM and want to improve its understanding of legal terminology.\n\nWe collect a large corpus containing:\n\n- Legal documents.\n- Court judgments.\n- Legal research papers.\n- Contracts and regulations.\n\nWe continue training the existing model on this text.\n\nThe model can learn additional domain-specific language patterns and terminology.\n\n**Use cases:**\n- Domain adaptation.\n- Learning specialised terminology.\n- Adapting models to a particular language or field.\n\n### C. RLHF (Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback)\n\nRLHF is a technique used to align an LLM's responses with human preferences.\n\nInstead of only learning from fixed correct answers, the model learns which responses humans prefer.\n\n**Basic workflow:**\n\n1. **Supervised Fine-Tuning:** First, the model is trained on high-quality instruction-response examples.\n2. **Human Feedback:** The model generates multiple responses to the same prompt. Human evaluators rank these responses based on quality, helpfulness, accuracy, and other criteria.\n3. **Reward Model:** A reward model is trained to predict which responses humans prefer.\n4. **Reinforcement Learning:** The language model is optimised using the reward model's feedback. PPO (Proximal Policy Optimization) is one traditional approach.\n\n**Example:**\n\nQuestion: Explain machine learning.\n\nResponse A: A clear, accurate explanation with an example.\n\nResponse B: An irrelevant and confusing explanation.\n\nHuman evaluators prefer Response A. The feedback helps train the model to produce responses that better match human preferences.\n\n**Use cases:**\n- Improving helpfulness.\n- Improving instruction following.\n- Reducing undesirable responses.\n- Aligning model behaviour with human preferences.\n\nNote: RLHF is not the only alignment approach. Methods such as DPO (Direct Preference Optimization) can optimise models using preference data without requiring a separately trained reward model and traditional reinforcement-learning loop.\n\n### D. LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation)\n\nLoRA is a parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) technique.\n\nIn traditional full fine-tuning, we update the model's original parameters. This can require substantial computational resources.\n\nLoRA reduces the number of trainable parameters.\n\n**How does LoRA work?**\n\n1. We start with a pre-trained model.\n2. We freeze its original weights.\n3. We introduce small trainable low-rank matrices into selected model layers.\n4. We train these additional matrices instead of updating all the original model parameters.\n\nThe original model remains frozen, while the LoRA adapters learn the required task-specific changes.\n\n**Advantages:**\n- Fewer trainable parameters.\n- Lower memory requirements.\n- Reduced training costs.\n- Easier to maintain multiple task-specific adapters.\n\n### E. QLoRA (Quantized Low-Rank Adaptation)\n\nQLoRA combines quantization with LoRA to make fine-tuning even more memory-efficient.\n\n**How does QLoRA work?**\n\n1. The pre-trained model is loaded in a quantized format, commonly 4-bit.\n2. The original quantized model weights remain frozen.\n3. LoRA adapters are added as trainable parameters.\n4. Only the adapters are trained.\n\nThis significantly reduces the memory required to fine-tune large language models.\n\n**Advantages:**\n- Lower GPU memory consumption.\n- Makes fine-tuning larger models more accessible.\n- Retains the parameter-efficient approach of LoRA.\n\n**Difference between LoRA and QLoRA:**\n\n| LoRA | QLoRA |\n|---|---|\n| Uses a frozen base model. | Uses a frozen quantized base model. |\n| Adds trainable low-rank adapters. | Adds trainable low-rank adapters. |\n| Base model weights are generally kept in their original precision. | Base model weights are commonly loaded in 4-bit precision. |\n| Reduces trainable parameters. | Reduces memory requirements further through quantization. |\n\n**Important distinction:** SFT, continued pre-training, and RLHF describe training objectives or approaches. LoRA and QLoRA describe parameter-efficient ways of implementing fine-tuning. They are not mutually exclusive. For example, we can perform SFT using LoRA or QLoRA.\n\n---\n\n## 3.2 How Do We Perform Fine-Tuning?\n\nA typical fine-tuning workflow consists of the following steps:\n\n### Step 1: Data Collection\n\nCollect a high-quality dataset relevant to the task or domain.\n\nThe dataset should be:\n- Relevant to the intended task.\n- Accurate and consistent.\n- Properly formatted.\n- Free from unnecessary or duplicate information.\n\n### Step 2: Choose a Fine-Tuning Method\n\nSelect the appropriate method depending on the objective and available resources.\n\nExamples:\n\n- SFT for instruction following.\n- Continued pre-training for domain adaptation.\n- RLHF for aligning model responses with human preferences.\n- LoRA for parameter-efficient fine-tuning.\n- QLoRA for memory-efficient fine-tuning.\n\n### Step 3: Train the Model\n\nPrepare the training data and configure the training process.\n\nTraining involves:\n- Selecting a suitable base model.\n- Preparing training and validation datasets.\n- Choosing hyperparameters such as learning rate and batch size.\n- Training for an appropriate number of epochs.\n- Monitoring the training and validation loss.\n\nIn full fine-tuning, the original model weights are updated.\n\nIn LoRA and QLoRA, the base model weights are frozen and the additional adapter parameters are trained.\n\n### Step 4: Evaluation and Safety Checks\n\nAfter training, evaluate the fine-tuned model.\n\nCheck:\n- Accuracy on unseen data.\n- Performance on the target task.\n- Generalisation ability.\n- Whether the model has overfitted.\n- Whether the model produces harmful or unreliable responses.\n\nIf the model performs poorly, we may need to improve the dataset, adjust hyperparameters, or select a different training method.\n\n### Additional Considerations\n\nFine-tuning is not a one-time guarantee of good performance. The model must be evaluated, monitored, and updated when necessary.\n\nWe should also maintain a separate test dataset to measure whether the fine-tuned model performs well on examples it has never seen.\n\n---\n\n## 3.3 What Problems Can Fine-Tuning Solve?\n\nFine-tuning can help with:\n\n1. **Domain-Specific Tasks:** It can improve the model's understanding of specialised domains such as law, medicine, finance, and programming.\n2. **Private or Proprietary Knowledge:** We can train a model using appropriately prepared private datasets, allowing it to learn relevant patterns and information.\n3. **Response Style:** It can teach a model to follow a particular tone, format, or response structure.\n4. **Task-Specific Behaviour:** It can improve performance on classification, extraction, summarisation, and other specialised tasks.\n\n### Can Fine-Tuning Solve All Three Problems?\n\nLet's revisit our original limitations.\n\n**1. Private data:** Fine-tuning can help a model learn from private data, but it does not guarantee accurate recall of specific documents or facts. It may also be difficult to update or remove individual facts.\n\n**2. Recent events:** Fine-tuning can incorporate newer information, but whenever the information changes, we may need to update the model again. This can become expensive and time-consuming.\n\n**3. Hallucination:** Fine-tuning can improve response quality and reduce certain types of hallucination, but it cannot eliminate hallucinations. A fine-tuned model can still generate incorrect information.\n\n## 3.4 Limitations of Fine-Tuning\n\n1. **Computationally Expensive:** Full fine-tuning of large models requires significant GPU memory and computational resources. LoRA and QLoRA reduce these requirements.\n\n2. **Requires Expertise:** Fine-tuning involves dataset preparation, model selection, training configuration, evaluation, and deployment. It requires technical knowledge.\n\n3. **Difficult to Maintain Frequently Changing Knowledge:** If we work in a domain where information changes rapidly, repeatedly fine-tuning the model may not be practical.\n\n4. **Risk of Overfitting:** If the model is trained on a small or repetitive dataset, it may memorise the training examples instead of learning patterns that generalise to new data.\n\n5. **Knowledge Updating Is Difficult:** Updating or removing a particular fact from a fine-tuned model is not as straightforward as editing a document in a database.\n\nSo, is there another way to provide an LLM with additional information without modifying its parameters?\n\nYes. This brings us to In-Context Learning.\n\n---\n\n# 4. In-Context Learning (ICL)\n\nIn-Context Learning is the ability of an LLM to perform a task by using instructions and examples provided directly in the prompt, without updating its model parameters.\n\nThe model uses the information available in the current context to understand what is expected and generate an appropriate response.\n\nThis capability is associated with large language models such as GPT, Llama, and Claude.\n\n### Example\n\nSuppose we want an LLM to classify customer feedback as positive or negative.\n\nInstead of fine-tuning the model, we provide examples directly in the prompt.\n\n**Prompt:**\n\nClassify the following sentences as Positive or Negative.\n\nExample 1:\n\nInput: I love this product.\n\nOutput: Positive.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: The product quality is terrible.\n\nOutput: Negative.\n\nNow provide a new sentence:\n\nInput: The product is amazing.\n\nThe model can infer the expected pattern from the examples and respond:\n\nOutput: Positive.\n\nNotice that we did not train the model again or update its weights. We simply provided examples in the prompt.\n\n## 4.1 Types of In-Context Learning\n\nIn-Context Learning is commonly categorised into three types:\n\n### A. Zero-Shot Learning\n\nWe provide instructions to the model without giving any examples.\n\nExample:\n\nPrompt: Classify the following sentence as Positive or Negative: \"I love this product.\"\n\nThe model understands the task using its existing capabilities.\n\n### B. One-Shot Learning\n\nWe provide one example before asking the model to perform the task.\n\nExample:\n\nExample:\n\nInput: I hate this product.\n\nOutput: Negative.\n\nNow classify:\n\nInput: I love this product.\n\nOutput: Positive.\n\n### C. Few-Shot Learning\n\nWe provide multiple examples in the prompt to demonstrate the desired behaviour.\n\nExample:\n\nInput: I love this product.\n\nOutput: Positive.\n\nInput: This is a terrible experience.\n\nOutput: Negative.\n\nInput: The service was excellent.\n\nOutput: Positive.\n\nNow classify:\n\nInput: The delivery was very disappointing.\n\nOutput: Negative.\n\n**Key Difference:**\n\n| Type | Number of Examples |\n|---|---:|\n| Zero-shot | 0 |\n| One-shot | 1 |\n| Few-shot | More than 1 |\n\n## 4.2 What Is an Emergent Property?\n\nAn emergent property is a capability or behaviour that becomes apparent in a system as it grows in scale or complexity, even though that capability may not be explicitly programmed as a separate feature.\n\nFor example, few-shot learning abilities become much more apparent in sufficiently large language models.\n\nHowever, not every capability is necessarily emergent in a strict scientific sense. Some abilities improve gradually with model scale, and the appearance of emergence can depend on how performance is measured.\n\n## 4.3 Why Is In-Context Learning Important?\n\n- No additional model training is required.\n- We do not need to update model parameters.\n- We can quickly change the task by changing the prompt.\n- We can provide examples and instructions dynamically.\n- It is useful when we want to adapt an LLM to different tasks without fine-tuning.\n\n### Limitation of In-Context Learning\n\nIn-context learning is not equally effective for every task.\n\nThe model is limited by its context window, and providing examples does not guarantee that it will understand or perform the task correctly.\n\nAlso, the model may not have enough information to answer questions about private documents, lengthy lectures, or recent events.\n\n### What If We Provide the Complete Relevant Context Instead of Just a Few Examples?\n\nLet's return to our lecture example.\n\nSuppose a student is watching a lecture and develops a doubt at a particular point.\n\nInstead of asking the LLM only:\n\n\"Explain this concept.\"\n\nWe can provide:\n\n1. The student's question.\n2. The transcript of the relevant part of the lecture.\n3. Any additional information needed to understand the question.\n\nThe LLM can now use the supplied context to generate a more relevant answer.\n\nWe are not updating the model's parameters. We are supplying additional information at inference time.\n\nThis idea leads us to Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG).\n\n---\n\n# 5. Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)\n\nRAG stands for **Retrieval-Augmented Generation**.\n\nRAG is a technique that improves an LLM's responses by retrieving relevant information from an external knowledge source and providing that information to the LLM as additional context while generating a response.\n\nIn simple words:\n\n**RAG makes an LLM more informed by giving it relevant external information at the time of answering a question.**\n\nUnlike fine-tuning, RAG does not require retraining the model or updating its parameters whenever we add new information.\n\n### Example: Understanding a Lecture\n\nSuppose a student is watching a lecture on RAG.\n\nAt a particular point in the video, the student has a doubt:\n\n\"What is the purpose of text chunking?\"\n\nThe LLM may not know the exact contents of that particular lecture.\n\nHowever, we can provide the transcript of the lecture as an external knowledge source.\n\nThe system can:\n\n1. Take the lecture transcript and prepare it for searching.\n2. Retrieve the relevant portion discussing text chunking.\n3. Combine the retrieved information with the student's question.\n4. Send the combined information to the LLM.\n5. Generate an answer based on the lecture content.\n\nThis is the basic idea behind RAG.\n\n## 5.1 Why Do We Need RAG?\n\nRAG helps address the limitations of relying only on parametric knowledge.\n\n### 1. Private or Domain-Specific Data\n\nWe can connect an LLM application to private documents, company records, lecture transcripts, PDFs, internal wikis, and other knowledge sources.\n\nThe model can retrieve relevant information from these sources when answering questions.\n\n### 2. Recent or Frequently Changing Information\n\nWe can update the external knowledge base whenever new information becomes available.\n\nFor example, if a company updates its internal policies, we can update the documents in the knowledge base instead of fine-tuning the entire LLM again.\n\nThe next time someone asks a question, the retriever can access the updated information.\n\n**Important:** RAG is only as up to date as its external knowledge source. The data must be refreshed, indexed, and made retrievable.\n\n### 3. Hallucination\n\nRAG can reduce hallucinations by providing relevant source information to the LLM.\n\nWe can also instruct the LLM to answer only from the retrieved context and say that it does not know when sufficient information is unavailable.\n\nHowever, RAG does not completely eliminate hallucinations. The model may still misunderstand the retrieved content or generate unsupported claims.\n\n---\n\n# 6. Working of RAG\n\nBroadly, the RAG pipeline consists of four major stages:\n\n1. Indexing\n2. Retrieval\n3. Augmentation\n4. Generation\n\n**Important:** Indexing is generally performed before a user asks a question. Retrieval, augmentation, and generation are performed when the user submits a query.\n\n---\n\n## 6.1 Indexing\n\nIndexing is the process of preparing a knowledge base so that it can be efficiently searched when a user asks a question.\n\nIt involves preparing documents, converting them into embeddings, and storing the resulting vectors along with their original text and metadata.\n\nIndexing consists of four important sub-steps.\n\n### Step 1: Document Ingestion\n\nDocument ingestion is the process of loading source knowledge into the system.\n\nWe first collect the information that we want our LLM application to access.\n\n**Examples of data sources:**\n\n- PDF reports and Word documents.\n- YouTube transcripts.\n- Blog posts and web pages.\n- GitHub repositories.\n- Internal company wikis.\n- SQL records.\n- Research papers.\n\n**Tools: LangChain Document Loaders**\n\nSome examples include:\n\n- `PyPDFLoader` – Loads content from PDF documents.\n- `YoutubeLoader` – Loads YouTube video transcripts where available.\n- `WebBaseLoader` – Loads content from web pages.\n- `GitLoader` – Loads content from Git repositories.\n\nA document loader generally converts the source data into a standard document representation that can be processed further.\n\nIn LangChain, a Document typically contains:\n\n- `page_content`: The actual text content.\n- `metadata`: Additional information about the document, such as its source, page number, or other identifying details.\n\n**Example:**\n\nA PDF loader reads a PDF and extracts its text into Document objects.\n\nAt this stage, we have loaded the original source information. We have not yet converted it into embeddings.\n\n### Step 2: Text Chunking\n\nText chunking is the process of breaking large documents into smaller, semantically meaningful pieces called chunks.\n\nInstead of processing an entire large document as one unit, we divide it into smaller sections.\n\n**Why do we need chunking?**\n\n1. **Context Window Limitations:** LLMs have a maximum context window. Sending an entire large document may exceed the available token limit.\n\n2. **Better Retrieval:** Smaller, focused chunks make it easier to retrieve information relevant to a particular query.\n\n3. **Reduced Irrelevant Information:** Retrieving a small relevant section is often more useful than retrieving an entire document.\n\n4. **Better Context Management:** We can provide only the most relevant chunks to the LLM instead of sending unnecessary content.\n\n**Example:**\n\nSuppose we have a document containing 10,000 words.\n\nInstead of storing it as one large document, we divide it into smaller chunks.\n\nFor example:\n\n- Chunk 1: Introduction to RAG.\n- Chunk 2: Limitations of LLMs.\n- Chunk 3: Document ingestion.\n- Chunk 4: Text chunking.\n- Chunk 5: Embedding generation.\n\nEach chunk can be processed and searched independently.\n\n**Common Text Splitters in LangChain:**\n\n- `RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter`\n- `MarkdownHeaderTextSplitter`\n- `SemanticChunker`\n\n**Types of Chunking:**\n\n1. **Length-Based Chunking:** Divides text based on a predefined character or token limit.\n2. **Structure-Based Chunking:** Divides text according to document structure, such as paragraphs, headings, or sections.\n3. **Semantic Chunking:** Divides text based on changes in meaning or semantic similarity.\n\n**What Is Chunk Overlap?**\n\nChunk overlap means keeping a small portion of the previous chunk in the next chunk.\n\nFor example:\n\nChunk 1: A B C D E\n\nChunk 2: D E F G H\n\nHere, D and E are repeated in both chunks.\n\n**Why do we need overlap?**\n\nSometimes, an important sentence or concept gets divided between two chunks.\n\nOverlap helps preserve contextual continuity and reduces the chances of losing important information at chunk boundaries.\n\nHowever, excessive overlap increases storage requirements and can produce redundant retrieval results.\n\n### Step 3: Embedding Generation\n\nAfter chunking, we convert each chunk into a numerical representation called an embedding.\n\nAn embedding is a dense numerical vector that captures semantic information about the text.\n\nIn simple words, embeddings convert text into numbers so that a machine can compare the semantic similarity between different pieces of text.\n\n**Example:**\n\nSuppose we have two sentences:\n\nSentence 1: \"I love machine learning.\"\n\nSentence 2: \"I enjoy studying ML.\"\n\nAlthough the sentences use different words, their meanings are similar.\n\nAn embedding model can convert both sentences into vectors that are relatively close to each other in the embedding space.\n\n**Why do we need embeddings?**\n\n1. Similar meanings can be represented by nearby vectors.\n2. They allow semantic search rather than relying only on exact keyword matching.\n3. They help retrieve relevant information even when the query uses different words from the original document.\n\n**Example:**\n\nStored text:\n\n\"Python is a programming language.\"\n\nUser query:\n\n\"Which language is used for software development?\"\n\nA keyword-based search may not find an exact match.\n\nHowever, semantic search can identify that the two pieces of text are related in meaning.\n\n**Common Embedding Models and Integrations:**\n\n- `OpenAIEmbeddings`\n- `HuggingFaceEmbeddings`\n- `SentenceTransformerEmbeddings`\n- `InstructEmbeddings`\n\nThe embedding model converts each chunk into a vector.\n\nFor example:\n\nText:\n\n\"Machine learning is a subset of artificial intelligence.\"\n\nEmbedding:\n\n`[0.12, -0.45, 0.78, 0.23, ...]`\n\nThe actual embedding can contain hundreds or thousands of dimensions, depending on the model.\n\n### Step 4: Storage in a Vector Store\n\nAfter generating embeddings, we need to store them somewhere so that they can be retrieved efficiently later.\n\nThis is where a vector store or vector database comes into the picture.\n\nA vector store stores:\n\n1. The embedding vectors.\n2. The original chunk text.\n3. Associated metadata.\n\n**Why store original text along with embeddings?**\n\nThe embedding is primarily used to find semantically similar information.\n\nOnce a relevant vector is found, we need the original text corresponding to that vector so that it can be passed to the LLM as context.\n\nMetadata can also help us identify the source of the retrieved information.\n\nFor example:\n\n- Document name.\n- Page number.\n- URL.\n- Timestamp.\n- Author.\n- Document category.\n\n**Vector Store Options:**\n\nLocal options:\n\n- FAISS\n- Chroma\n\nCloud and managed options:\n\n- Pinecone\n- Weaviate\n- Milvus\n- Qdrant\n\nThe vector store acts as the searchable external knowledge base for the RAG application.\n\n**Important distinction:**\n\nA vector store is a software component that stores and retrieves vectors. A vector database is a database system designed to manage vector data and support vector search, often with additional capabilities such as persistence, metadata filtering, and scaling.\n\nThe terms are sometimes used interchangeably in RAG tutorials, but they are not always identical.\n\n### Summary of Indexing\n\nThe complete indexing pipeline looks like this:\n\n1. Document Loader → Loads the original documents.\n2. Text Splitter → Divides documents into smaller chunks.\n3. Embedding Model → Converts chunks into numerical vectors.\n4. Vector Store → Stores vectors along with original text and metadata.\n\nAt the end of indexing, our external knowledge base is ready to be searched.\n\n---\n\n## 6.2 Retrieval\n\nRetrieval is the real-time process of finding the most relevant pieces of information from the pre-built knowledge base based on the user's query.\n\nIn simple words:\n\nRetrieval finds the chunks that are most useful for answering the user's question.\n\n**Example:**\n\nSuppose our vector database contains thousands of chunks from a RAG lecture.\n\nThe user asks:\n\n\"Why do we need text chunking?\"\n\nThe retriever searches the vector store and returns the chunks that are most relevant to this question.\n\nIt is essentially asking:\n\n\"From all the information available in my knowledge base, which 3–5 chunks are most helpful for answering this query?\"\n\n### How Does Retrieval Work?\n\n1. The user submits a query.\n2. The query is converted into an embedding using the embedding model.\n3. The query embedding is compared with the stored document embeddings.\n4. The system identifies the most similar vectors.\n5. The corresponding original text chunks are retrieved.\n\n### Similarity Search\n\nSimilarity search is used to identify vectors that are close to the query vector in the embedding space.\n\nCommon similarity measures include:\n\n- Cosine Similarity.\n- Dot Product.\n- Euclidean Distance.\n\n**Cosine Similarity:**\n\nCosine similarity measures the cosine of the angle between two vectors.\n\nIt is commonly used to measure how similar two text embeddings are.\n\nA higher cosine similarity generally indicates greater directional similarity between the vectors.\n\n### Top-K Retrieval\n\nTop-K retrieval means returning the K most relevant chunks for a query.\n\nFor example, if K = 3, the retriever returns the three most relevant chunks.\n\nChoosing K is important:\n\n- Too few chunks may result in missing important information.\n- Too many chunks may introduce irrelevant information and consume the LLM's context window.\n\n### Types of Retrieval\n\nSome commonly used retrieval approaches include:\n\n1. **Similarity Search:** Retrieves chunks based on vector similarity.\n2. **MMR (Maximum Marginal Relevance):** Retrieves relevant chunks while also trying to reduce redundancy among the selected results.\n3. **Hybrid Search:** Combines semantic vector search with keyword-based search.\n4. **Metadata Filtering:** Restricts retrieval using metadata such as document type, date, author, or category.\n\nA more advanced RAG system may also use a reranker to reorder retrieved documents based on their relevance to the query.\n\n---\n\n## 6.3 Augmentation\n\nAugmentation is the process of combining the user's original query with the relevant information retrieved from the external knowledge base.\n\nThe retrieved chunks are added to the prompt as additional context.\n\nThe purpose is to provide the LLM with the information it needs before generating an answer.\n\n**Example:**\n\nUser Query:\n\n\"Why do we need text chunking?\"\n\nRetrieved Context:\n\n\"Text chunking divides large documents into smaller, semantically meaningful pieces. Smaller chunks make semantic retrieval more focused and help manage LLM context window limitations.\"\n\nNow we combine both.\n\n**Augmented Prompt:**\n\nYou are a helpful assistant.\n\nAnswer the question ONLY using the provided context.\n\nIf the context is insufficient, say that you don't know.\n\nContext:\n\n\"Text chunking divides large documents into smaller, semantically meaningful pieces. Smaller chunks make semantic retrieval more focused and help manage LLM context window limitations.\"\n\nQuestion:\n\n\"Why do we need text chunking?\"\n\nThis combined prompt is sent to the LLM.\n\n**Why is augmentation important?**\n\n- It gives the LLM access to external knowledge.\n- It provides relevant information related to the user's query.\n- It helps ground the response in the retrieved source material.\n- It reduces the need for the LLM to rely entirely on its parametric knowledge.\n\n---\n\n## 6.4 Generation\n\nGeneration is the final stage of the RAG pipeline.\n\nAt this stage, the LLM receives the augmented prompt containing:\n\n1. The user's original question.\n2. The retrieved context.\n3. Instructions describing how to generate the answer.\n\nThe LLM processes this information and generates a response.\n\n**Example:**\n\nQuestion:\n\n\"Why do we need text chunking?\"\n\nRetrieved Context:\n\n\"Text chunking divides large documents into smaller, semantically meaningful pieces. Smaller chunks make semantic retrieval more focused and help manage LLM context window limitations.\"\n\nGenerated Answer:\n\n\"Text chunking is important because it divides large documents into smaller, manageable pieces. This makes semantic retrieval more focused and helps us work within the context window limitations of LLMs.\"\n\nThe answer is now based on both the user's query and the retrieved external knowledge.\n\n**Important:** The LLM is still generating the response using its language-generation capabilities. RAG does not replace the LLM. It provides additional information that the LLM can use while generating its response.\n\n---\n\n# 7. Complete RAG Workflow\n\nLet's connect all four stages.\n\n### Before the User Asks a Question: Indexing\n\n1. Collect documents using document loaders.\n2. Divide the documents into smaller chunks.\n3. Convert the chunks into embeddings.\n4. Store embeddings, original text, and metadata in a vector store.\n\nThis creates the external knowledge base.\n\n### When the User Asks a Question\n\n**Step 1: Retrieval**\n\nThe user's query is converted into an embedding, and the retriever searches the vector store for relevant chunks.\n\n**Step 2: Augmentation**\n\nThe retrieved chunks are combined with the original query to construct an augmented prompt.\n\n**Step 3: Generation**\n\nThe LLM uses the augmented prompt to generate the final response.\n\n### Complete Pipeline\n\nIndexing:\n\nDocuments → Document Loader → Text Splitter → Embedding Model → Vector Store\n\nQuery Processing:\n\nUser Query → Query Embedding → Retriever → Relevant Chunks\n\nAnswer Generation:\n\nRelevant Chunks + User Query → Augmented Prompt → LLM → Final Answer\n\n---\n\n# 8. Fine-Tuning vs In-Context Learning vs RAG\n\nThese three techniques are important, but they solve different problems.\n\n| Feature | Fine-Tuning | In-Context Learning | RAG |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Main idea | Further train a model | Provide instructions and examples in the prompt | Retrieve external knowledge and provide it as context |\n| Updates model weights? | Yes, in full fine-tuning; adapters in PEFT | No | No |\n| Requires training? | Yes | No | No additional LLM training required |\n| Uses external information? | Through training data | Through prompt content | Through a retrievable knowledge base |\n| Updating knowledge | Usually requires further training | Change the prompt | Update and re-index the knowledge base |\n| Best suited for | Task adaptation and behaviour | Quick task adaptation | Knowledge-intensive question answering |\n\n### When Should We Use Each?\n\n**Use Fine-Tuning when:**\n\n- We want to improve task-specific performance.\n- We want a consistent response style or format.\n- We need domain-specific behavioural adaptation.\n- We have sufficient high-quality training data.\n\n**Use In-Context Learning when:**\n\n- We want to provide examples without training.\n- We need to change instructions dynamically.\n- The task can be explained through a prompt.\n- We have a small amount of relevant context to provide.\n\n**Use RAG when:**\n\n- We need answers based on private documents.\n- We work with frequently updated information.\n- We need source-grounded responses.\n- We want to retrieve information from a large external knowledge base.\n\n**Can We Combine Fine-Tuning and RAG?**\n\nYes.\n\nFor example, we can fine-tune an LLM to improve its ability to follow instructions and format answers, while using RAG to provide up-to-date company information.\n\nFine-tuning improves how the model behaves, while RAG provides relevant external knowledge.\n\n---\n\n# 9. Advantages of RAG\n\n1. **Access to Private Data:** Allows LLM applications to answer questions using private documents and proprietary information.\n\n2. **Updated Knowledge:** The knowledge base can be updated without retraining the LLM.\n\n3. **Reduced Hallucination:** Providing relevant source information can reduce unsupported responses.\n\n4. **Source Attribution:** Retrieved documents can be used to provide references or citations in the generated answer.\n\n5. **Cost Efficiency:** Updating documents and embeddings can be more practical than repeatedly fine-tuning a large model.\n\n6. **Domain-Specific Applications:** RAG can be used to build specialised assistants for healthcare, education, finance, customer support, and enterprise applications.\n\n# 10. Limitations of RAG\n\nRAG is powerful, but it is not a perfect solution.\n\n1. **Retrieval Quality:** If the retriever fails to find the correct chunks, the LLM may generate an incorrect or incomplete answer.\n\n2. **Chunking Challenges:** Poor chunk sizes or chunk boundaries can remove important contextual information.\n\n3. **Embedding Quality:** A weak or unsuitable embedding model may fail to capture the semantic meaning of the documents.\n\n4. **Latency:** RAG introduces additional steps such as embedding generation, vector search, and retrieval before the LLM generates a response.\n\n5. **Context Window Limitations:** Even after retrieval, the amount of context provided to the LLM is limited.\n\n6. **Data Quality:** If the knowledge base contains outdated, incorrect, or contradictory information, RAG may retrieve and use that information.\n\n7. **Hallucinations Are Still Possible:** The LLM may misunderstand retrieved information or generate claims that are not supported by the context.\n\n8. **Maintenance:** The knowledge base, embedding pipeline, and retrieval system need to be maintained and monitored.\n\n---\n\n# 11. Important Terms to Remember\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Parametric Knowledge | Knowledge encoded in the model's learned parameters. |\n| Fine-Tuning | Further training a pre-trained model for a specific task or domain. |\n| SFT | Training a model on labelled input-output examples. |\n| Continued Pre-Training | Further pre-training on additional unlabelled text data. |\n| RLHF | Aligning model responses using human preference feedback. |\n| LoRA | Parameter-efficient fine-tuning using low-rank trainable adapters. |\n| QLoRA | LoRA-based fine-tuning with a quantized base model. |\n| In-Context Learning | Learning to perform a task from instructions and examples in the prompt without updating weights. |\n| Zero-Shot | Performing a task without examples. |\n| One-Shot | Performing a task with one example. |\n| Few-Shot | Performing a task using multiple examples. |\n| Emergent Property | A capability or behaviour that becomes apparent as a system's scale or complexity increases. |\n| RAG | Retrieving external information and providing it to an LLM as context. |\n| Indexing | Preparing documents for efficient retrieval. |\n| Document Ingestion | Loading source documents into a system. |\n| Chunking | Dividing large documents into smaller pieces. |\n| Embedding | Numerical representation of text that captures semantic information. |\n| Vector Store | A component used to store and retrieve vector embeddings. |\n| Retrieval | Finding relevant information from a knowledge base. |\n| Augmentation | Combining retrieved information with the user's query. |\n| Generation | Producing the final response using the augmented prompt. |\n| Semantic Search | Searching based on meaning rather than exact keyword matches. |\n| Top-K | Retrieving the K most relevant results. |\n| Metadata | Additional information associated with a document or chunk. |\n\n---\n\n# 12. Final Understanding\n\nAn LLM stores the knowledge learned during pre-training in its parameters, which is called parametric knowledge.\n\nHowever, relying only on this knowledge creates limitations when dealing with private information, recent events, and hallucinations.\n\nWe have three important approaches:\n\n1. **Fine-Tuning:** Further trains the model to improve its behaviour or performance on specific tasks and domains.\n2. **In-Context Learning:** Provides instructions and examples directly in the prompt without updating the model's weights.\n3. **RAG:** Retrieves relevant external information from a knowledge base and supplies it to the LLM during inference.\n\nThe most important distinction is:\n\n**Fine-Tuning changes the model. In-Context Learning changes the prompt. RAG changes the information available to the model at inference time.**\n\nRAG is particularly useful when we want an LLM to answer questions using large amounts of private, domain-specific, or frequently updated information without repeatedly retraining the model.","metadata":{}}]}